# Assessment: Deliberative Learning: Judgment in Place of a Value Function

**Proposed purpose (the operator named none):** machine-learning and
agent-systems researchers who do not know Commonplace should be able to state
the article's two-level claim, follow it without internal vocabulary, and see
what evidence would decide the bet. The two levels are: deliberative learning
is a learning paradigm with a judgment in the selection role, and a
fixed-weight language model can fill that judge role.

No write brief is declared. No earlier decisions were supplied. The role
report finds no inbound dependents, so no planned change alters a claim or
term that another artifact relies on.

## Verdict on stopping

No stop. All three depth 1 reports find the core mechanism standing: the
friction filter SURVIVES, the premise decomposition finds every dent LOCAL,
and the critique "partially lands". The substantive problems are defects that
one or two sentences can correct at separate joints, plus choices and
extensions. Each is listed under *Claim questions for the operator*, and the
passes are scoped away from those passages.

The grounding-alignment FAIL does not mean the claims are wrong. It means
the Minton and Harness Evolution ingests retain no quotes. The repair is
quote retention with `cp-skill-ground` on those ingests, not a rewrite of
this artifact (see below). The passes do not touch the PRODIGY paragraph or
the Harness Evolution sentence.

## What reads well

The routing-line walkthrough (admission, targeted narrowing, then revision of
the review check) makes the abstract paradigm concrete before the definition.
The evidence section is candid that no end-to-end automated run exists.

## The sentence a hostile reviewer attacks first

"Looking at three precedents shows that the bottleneck was always **what
played the value-function role**." Six reviews converge on it. The article's
own EURISKO paragraph attributes that system's limit to brittle heuristics and
human intervention. The cited Harness Evolution data places a stall on the
proposer's inputs. Three selected cases cannot show "always". The section
heading also names three precedents, while the body gives four bolded entries.
This is a claim question, not a pass.

