## Claim questions for the operator

Each item below is a defect that one or two sentences would correct, or a
choice. No pass touches these passages.

1. **Bottleneck "always".** This is a defect: two statements that cannot both
   hold. "three precedents shows that the bottleneck was always what played
   the value-function role" conflicts with "EURISKO's symbolic heuristics
   remained brittle and repeatedly needed Lenat to intervene". The cited
   Harness Evolution data also places the stall on proposer inputs. A
   possible correction is "suggests … was a recurring bottleneck", with the
   proposer named as a rival locus. The enumeration is stale: "three
   precedents" against four bolded entries, one of which (on-balance
   judgment) is not a precedent. The grounding review also finds the EURISKO
   "brittle … Lenat" characterization unsupported by the retained quotes.
   Reports: `…/semantic/epistemic-status-blur.md`,
   `…/sentence/framing-mismatch.md`, `…/semantic/internal-consistency.md`,
   `kb/instructions/premise-decomposition-gate.md` result,
   `kb/instructions/critique-note.md` result,
   `kb/instructions/composition-friction-gate.md` result,
   `…/semantic/grounding-alignment.md`.
2. **Falsification clause.** This is a defect: a case inside the stated
   conditions for which the claim is false. "the bet fails—and weights-only
   continual learning wins those domains". If the LLM judge fails, human-judged
   loops, fixed-criterion loops or hybrids remain. The cited study itself
   reports an adapter that stacks with an evolved harness. The comparison
   basis, the baseline and the scope of "those domains" are also unspecified.
   Possible correction: stop at "the bet fails", or name weights-only learning
   as the main rival rather than the winner. Reports: completeness,
   underspecified-assertions, `kb/articles/COLLECTION.md` result,
   framing-mismatch, friction, premise decomposition and critique.
3. **"That sequence establishes three things".** This is a defect: two
   statements that cannot both hold. A constructed hypothetical ("Suppose …")
   is said to establish outcomes, while the evidence section says such a
   verdict stays open and reports no completed run. Possible correction:
   "illustrates". Reports: epistemic-status-blur and friction.
4. **Admission credited to "an on-balance judgment".** This is a defect: two
   readings. The example's recap credits the admission to "an on-balance
   judgment" that "played the role a scalar loss … plays". The admission was
   in fact a bounded decision justified by reversibility under an open
   verdict. The example also frames reversibility as "cheap to revert". The
   linked reversibility note says the bound comes from detection and
   correction time, not revert cost. Reports: critique,
   internal-consistency (INFO) and framing-mismatch.
5. **Two formulations of the bet.** This is a choice; I cannot tell defect
   from extension, so I treat it as a choice. The opening states "an
   automated machine learning paradigm" that scales with computation. The
   bold sentence states autonomous double-loop learning with targeted
   correction where no fixed criterion is adequate. The evidence section's
   test fits neither fully: it checks per-change operative dependence and
   improvement, not criterion revision or autonomy (internal-consistency
   WARN). Decide which formulation the article commits to, and either add a
   check for criterion revision or say that the two-part test covers only
   part of the bet. Reports: the synthesis (run dir `…/20261121T192511Z/synthesis`,
   opportunity 1) and `…/semantic/internal-consistency.md`.
6. **The value-function slot.** This is a choice; no defect is shown, because
   the opening stipulates its own sense. Conceptual-role-conflation asks
   whether "value function" is the imported RL concept or the article's
   broader stipulated sense. The premise decomposition argues that the judge
   admits or rejects proposals the proposer generates, which matches a
   selection or acceptance criterion, not a gradient loss. It suggests "in
   place of a selection criterion", which changes the title and file name.
   This lies outside `repair-claim-title`. The critique adds that the success
   test ("every reasonable measure") makes the judge a non-scalarizing
   dominance check. If so, the article should say what stays distinct.
   "Every machine learning loop needs … a selection signal … that decides
   which updates stay" is also strained by SGD and by the article's own
   direct-update examples.
7. **Bitter Lesson placement.** This is a choice. "the system sits on the
   computation side of the Bitter Lesson" is stated as a consequence, though
   it belongs to the bet. The friction and premise reports argue that a
   fixed-weight judge caps reliability, so scaling must be part of the bet
   rather than follow from placing an LLM in the loop. Possible correction:
   one clause marking it as part of the bet.
8. **Harness Evolution reading.** This is a defect: an epistemic overstatement
   and an ambiguous attachment. "its own data located the stall" presents a
   proposed mechanism as established. "a quarter of runs that never reached
   the proposer" attaches to "runs". Nothing in the article is retained as
   support, because the ingest has no quotes. The critique adds that a
   proposer-side stall cuts against the judge-side thesis (see 1). Decide the
   wording after quote retention.
9. **Comparison-table rows.** This is a defect: a case outside the row. The
   "When updates happen: Deployment time" row and the "Inspecting traces" row
   do not follow from the two defining conditions. Online weight updates
   happen at deployment, a team's pre-release handbook edits are deliberative
   learning outside it, and a one-shot admission gate has no trace-based
   correction. Possible correction: label the column as the intended deployed
   instance. Reports: completeness and premise decomposition.

